# LLMsVerifier — example environment
#
# Copy to `.env`, fill in the values your deployment needs, and keep the copy out
# of version control (`.env` and `*.env` are gitignored; this `.env.example` is the
# only committed variant and MUST NEVER contain a real value, key, or token).
#
#     cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# IMPORTANT — nothing in this repository auto-loads a `.env` file. These variables
# are read from the *process* environment at the moment configurations are
# generated. Export them in the generating shell, or feed the file to whatever
# starts the process:
#
#     set -a; . ./.env; set +a          # POSIX shell
#     env_file: [.env]                  # Docker / Podman Compose service
#     EnvironmentFile=/path/to/.env     # systemd unit
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HelixAgent endpoint injection
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The configurations this project generates for CLI agents point at a HelixAgent
# deployment (its MCP / ACP / LSP / embeddings / vision endpoints). LLMsVerifier is
# shared infrastructure and cannot know where your HelixAgent listens, so you inject
# that endpoint here. Full contract, including which variable reaches which generated
# artifact: see "HelixAgent Endpoint Configuration" in README.md.
#
# Resolution order, highest precedence first:
#   1. HELIX_AGENT_BASE_URL
#   2. HELIX_AGENT_HOST / HELIX_AGENT_PORT
#   3. the documented placeholder http://localhost:8100
#
# With none of them set, generated configs resolve to the placeholder
# `http://localhost:8100`. That is a syntactically valid last resort, NOT a working
# HelixAgent endpoint — inject your own rather than relying on it.

# A value you supply that cannot be used is never applied silently: a rejected host,
# a rejected port, and a base URL set without a usable host each print one warning to
# stderr when configurations are generated. Leaving a variable unset is not warned
# about — that is the supported zero-configuration path. See "Diagnostics on stderr"
# in README.md.
#
# Those warnings quote the rejected value so you can spot the typo, but only when it
# cannot carry a credential: a value containing `@`, `/` (so any `://` URL), `?`, `#`,
# any percent-escape (a `%` plus two hex digits, so an encoded `%40`/`%3A` is caught at
# any encoding depth), or a `:` whose right-hand side is not a plain port number is
# withheld and printed as `<value withheld: may carry credentials>`. HELIX_AGENT_BASE_URL
# is never echoed at all. The check matches shape, not secrecy — do not put credentials
# in HELIX_AGENT_HOST or HELIX_AGENT_PORT.

# A COMPLETE base URL, used verbatim (any trailing slash is trimmed). This is the
# only way to express a non-http scheme, a path prefix, or a host:port pair in one
# string. NOTE: it is honoured by the Crush provider base URL, but NOT by the
# CLI-agent MCP / skill / extension URLs — those follow HELIX_AGENT_HOST/PORT below.
# Set both if you use this one; setting only this one warns on stderr and leaves the
# CLI-agent configs pointing at the placeholder.
#HELIX_AGENT_BASE_URL=https://agent.internal.example/helix

# HOST ONLY — a hostname or IP literal. Never `host:port`: a colon is accepted only
# inside a genuine IPv6 literal, and anything else is rejected as malformed (the host
# then falls back to `localhost`, with a warning). IPv6 goes in bare, e.g. `::1`;
# brackets are added automatically in emitted URLs.
#HELIX_AGENT_HOST=agent.internal.example

# PORT ONLY — an integer in 1..65535. A non-numeric or out-of-range value falls back
# to the placeholder port (8100) with a warning, while a valid HELIX_AGENT_HOST is
# still honoured. Host and port fall back independently of each other.
# HelixAgent's own default port is 7061 at the time of writing — that is its current
# default, not a contract; use whatever port your deployment actually listens on.
#HELIX_AGENT_PORT=7061

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Trusted reverse-proxy allowlist (X-Forwarded-For / X-Real-IP)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read directly by llm-verifier/api/middleware.go's getClientIP, which feeds both
# rate limiting and the audit-log ClientIP field. UNSET BY DEFAULT — with no value
# here, X-Forwarded-For / X-Real-IP are ignored entirely and every caller's identity
# is its own raw TCP peer address. That default is deliberate ("deny by default":
# an unconfigured or forged forwarding header can never grant an attacker a spoofed
# identity) but it means that if you deploy behind a reverse proxy (this repo ships
# BOTH a direct-exposure quick-start docker-compose.yml with no proxy AND
# proxied production topologies in docker-compose.prod.yml / llm-verifier/
# docker-compose.yml / k8s-manifests.yaml) you MUST set this to your proxy's actual
# address/CIDR, or every user behind that proxy shares one rate-limit bucket — under
# real load that reads as every legitimate user getting 429s, i.e. an outage, even
# though nothing is actually broken. See "Trusted Proxies (X-Forwarded-For /
# X-Real-IP)" in llm-verifier/docs/ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLES.md for the full rationale,
# per-topology example values, and why no default value here would be safe (CONST-045
# forbids hardcoding a distribution host, and the correct value differs by topology).
#LLM_VERIFIER_TRUSTED_PROXIES=172.20.0.0/16

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Provider credentials
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Provider API keys are also supplied through the environment and referenced from
# `config.yaml` as `${VAR}` — see "Basic Configuration" in README.md for the config
# layout. Add the variables your enabled providers require to your own `.env`.
# Never place a real key in this example file.
